# The espflash both desktop apps bundle as their flash engine: esptool's Rust
# sibling, pinned so a board is only ever written by a flash engine we chose
# on purpose. Bump deliberately.
#
# ONE file, read by both release workflows — the SecuraCV Flasher's
# (.github/workflows/desktop-flasher-release.yml) and the SecuraCV Lab's
# (.github/workflows/desktop-release.yml), in their "Load the espflash pins"
# step — so the two apps can never ship different engines. Both apps'
# .github/release-targets.yml watches name this file, so a pin bump alone
# marks both apps as changed ("Update everything" asks for a version bump
# instead of reporting "nothing to do"). RELEASE_LESSONS 2026-09-23 (b).
#
# The sha256s are of each prebuilt espflash release asset for
# ESPFLASH_VERSION. GitHub release assets are MUTABLE (an asset can be
# replaced without the tag moving), and these binaries are bundled as
# sidecars inside the signed, self-updating apps, so both workflows verify
# each download against its pin and fail the release on any mismatch.
# Recompute every one when ESPFLASH_VERSION changes:
#   curl -fL https://github.com/esp-rs/espflash/releases/download/v<version>/espflash-<triple>.zip | sha256sum
#
# Format: KEY=value lines (no quotes, no spaces); blank lines and lines that
# start with `#` are ignored. The load step refuses any other line, a key
# pinned twice, a key missing, a version that isn't x.y.z and a digest that
# isn't 64 lowercase hex — and canary-local/tests/desktop_parity.test.js runs
# that step against this file on every PR that touches it.
ESPFLASH_VERSION=3.3.0
ESPFLASH_SHA256_AARCH64_APPLE_DARWIN=e2b6b7f1c48c89069c23b425ce9ed368b242de52e0080d31155bb220847f794a
ESPFLASH_SHA256_X86_64_APPLE_DARWIN=7b18f89e0e584da8024b09ac5ba9400481a505fc59b0ad15c47254adb50a5ded
ESPFLASH_SHA256_X86_64_UNKNOWN_LINUX_GNU=83611abe54c8e3d8c5a6b44948f4505b9cee05505577e2d9276e35a51e503d64
